		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=36100#p36100"><![CDATA[
Sequential Impulse is mathematically equivalent to Projected Gauss Seidel, it is just an implementation detail where to store the accumulated impulse (also known as delta velocity, acceleration or Lagrange multiplier).<br><br>Here is a screenshot of a slide of my recent presentation at SIGGRAPH that could help you clarifying it.<br>Either you re-compute the delta based on the body velocity (projected onto the normal/Jacobian) or you use the x vector.<br>When storing the applied impulse in the rigid body using apply impulse, you basically convert the impulse into world space. THen you have to project the velocity back in constraint space using the dot project. When using plain PGS, this conversion is not needed. <br><div class="inline-attachment"><dl class="file"><dt class="attach-image"><img src="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/download/file.php?id=1348" class="postimage" alt="SI_vs_PGS.png" onclick="viewableArea(this);" /></dt></dl></div><p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=2">Erwin Coumans</a> — Thu Aug 20, 2015 4:13 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
